    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "...pipeline can be customized to fit your specific needs, and it's worth noting that the \"Code Review\" gate can be particularly effective in preventing \"rogue\" code from making it into production. However, it's also a gate that can be exploited by a clever \"goblin\" of a developer who tries to sneak in changes under the radar.\n\nWhen it comes to bypassing quality gates, one of the biggest risks is the \"Code Slippery Slope\" – where a developer makes a small change, and then another developer builds upon that change without fully reviewing it, creating a snowball effect that can lead to a large number of issues making it into production.\n\nAnother risk is the \"Goblin in the Code\" – where a small, seemingly innocuous change is made, but ends up introducing a security vulnerability or performance issue that can have far-reaching consequences.\n\nIn terms of specific risks, some common ways that unauthorized changes might try to bypass quality gates include:\n\n* \"Code Injection\" – where a developer injects malicious code into the pipeline, either intentionally or unintentionally.\n* \"Code Tampering\" – where a developer modifies existing code without fully reviewing it, or modifies the pipeline itself to allow unauthorized changes to pass through.\n*",
